My Early Struggles and Adaptation: Finding My Footing at UET

Unfamiliar Territory

The first days at UET Faisalabad were a genuine test of character in ways I had not fully anticipated. I had prepared mentally for the academic challenge — new subjects, demanding faculty, higher standards. What I had not prepared for was the sheer volume of small, practical unknowns that come with starting life in a new city. Where to buy stationery. Which canteen had reasonable food. How the library borrowing system worked. What the unwritten rules of the student community were. None of these things seem significant in isolation, but when you are trying to figure out all of them simultaneously while also attending your first lectures and submitting your first registrations, they add up quickly into a daily experience that feels exhausting before the real work has even begun.

I arrived with enthusiasm and determination, but enthusiasm alone does not prepare you for that kind of layered adjustment. Every day brought new situations that required me to ask for help, observe how others navigated things, and accept that not knowing was a temporary condition I needed to move through rather than be ashamed of. This in itself was a lesson. At school, I had known how everything worked. The comfort of familiarity had allowed me to focus entirely on academics. At university, I had to develop competence in the environment itself before I could channel my energy fully into what I had come here to do.

Discipline and Structured Thinking

One of the most impactful early experiences was the way the faculty approached their subjects from the very first lectures. There was an immediate and clear message that university-level learning required a different quality of engagement. Professors did not slow down to accommodate gaps in foundational knowledge — they assumed a baseline and built from there. This was particularly challenging given that I was coming from a Pre-Medical background with limited exposure to mathematics, programming, and engineering concepts.

Among the faculty who made an immediate impression was Dr. Bilal Ahmad. In his Programming Fundamentals lectures, he had a way of structuring explanations that forced you to think rather than just receive information. He would present a problem, ask students how they would approach it, and then systematically work through the logic of a solution. This Socratic quality in his teaching made it impossible to be a passive listener. It was uncomfortable at times, especially early on when I did not yet have the vocabulary or the conceptual foundation to engage confidently. But it was exactly the kind of discomfort that produces real learning. Dr. Ahmad's background as an AI and machine learning researcher — someone who works on problems with genuine real-world consequences, particularly in medical fields — gave him a perspective that made even basic programming concepts feel connected to something meaningful.

Finding Balance and Moving Forward

The process of finding balance happened gradually, not suddenly. There was no single moment where everything clicked — rather, it was a series of small adjustments that accumulated into a more stable way of operating. I developed a daily schedule that accounted for lectures, self-study, meals, and rest. I began identifying which subjects needed more of my attention and allocating my time accordingly. I started treating confusion not as a sign of failure but as information about where to direct more effort. These habits sound simple, but building them from scratch in a new environment required genuine intentionality.

By the end of the first month, I was no longer just surviving the experience — I was beginning to understand it. The campus had become familiar. My friendships had deepened from casual conversation into genuine mutual support. And my academic engagement had shifted from anxious attendance to curious participation. The adaptation process was not over — it never fully is — but I had crossed an important threshold. I was no longer trying to make sense of everything at once. I was living inside the experience and growing from it day by day. That shift in orientation, from overwhelmed newcomer to engaged student, was the most important thing that happened in those first weeks at university.

